     1	Desktop-specific target assets belong here when a class helper needs files that
     2	must not apply to server installs. Shared target assets stay under
     3	`hooks/shared/target/`. Mirror the installed path directly under `target/`,
     4	for example `target/etc/...` or `target/usr/local/...`.
     5	
     6	Desktop-only Perl runtime modules are authoritative here, not in the shared
     7	hook tree: Whisper, Digital Assets, and managed external-software modules live
     8	under `target/usr/local/libexec/{whisper,digital-assets,external-managed-software}`.
     9	Their installed paths remain `/usr/local/libexec/...`; the desktop role owns
    10	their source staging and deployment.
    11	
    12	The security class stages `/etc/apparmor.d/managed-desktop-wrappers`, with one
    13	profile attachment for every installed desktop `bin` and `libexec`
    14	helper, including rendered session/root templates, all six desktop `sbin`
    15	wrappers, the shared XSSH send/retrieve wrappers, and the TPM2 enrollment
    16	launcher/helper pair. Internal helper calls transition to the matching managed
    17	profile through rules that also permit bounded preflight reads. Named targets
    18	are used unless a fan-out exceeds AppArmor's per-profile target limit, in which
    19	case the exact executable attachment resolves the same strict transition.
    20	`DESKTOP_APPARMOR_STATE` selects enforce or complain mode for the complete
    21	wrapper set and the default-enforced managed desktop application profiles
    22	during target staging.
    23	All managed GUI profiles share one Wayland/GLES/EGL/DRI/OpenGL policy with
    24	Mesa, Intel, NVIDIA, and shader-cache access. The shared AppArmor graphics
    25	abstraction permits the Vulkan loader ABI needed by package dependencies but
    26	explicitly denies Vulkan ICD paths, while the managed launchers prevent Vulkan
    27	feature or backend selection. GTK wrappers that render their own surfaces use
    28	the same graphics abstraction, and package-owned GUI
    29	profiles receive a generated `local/<profile>` include before their
    30	unconfined/default mode is changed, preventing incomplete compatibility
    31	profiles from flooding the AppArmor event log.
    32	Package applications and privileged tools do not inherit the shell
    33	wrapper domain; each wrapper receives only its bounded configuration, state,
    34	capture, report, or policy paths. The complete enforced set contains 65
    35	installed-path attachments when the selected desktop AppArmor state is enforce.
    36	FeatherPad, FocusWriter, KDiff3, Micro, Neovim, Qalculate, and Vim defaults are
    37	desktop-role assets and are staged only when the desktop role is selected.
    38	Recoll and Recollgui are installed with a multilingual, resource-bounded index
    39	baseline, XDG-cached index and OCR data, private per-user GUI and index
    40	configuration state, and GUI preferences which retain native desktop opening
    41	and system color handling. Credential stores, cloud/container authentication
    42	data, browser profiles, version-control metadata, removable mounts, and
    43	transient build trees are excluded. Only the KeePassXC subtree below
    44	`~/Syncthing` is excluded, so other synchronized documents remain searchable.
    45	Image-only PDFs use bounded Tesseract OCR with the installed English and Swedish
    46	language data, backed by Poppler extraction tools.
    47	
    48	The desktop role stages Labwc session assets, Waybar/Fuzzel/Mako/Kanshi user
    49	defaults, Zsh/Starship/fzf/btop defaults, portal and KWallet defaults, and a
    50	`labwc-dock` wrapper for `crystal-dock`. The Waybar `quick-controls` groups render
    51	network, Bluetooth, keyboard-layout, and screenshot modules inside one bordered
    52	pill with transparent, borderless child controls. Internal eDP, LVDS, and
    53	DSI outputs use a native output-specific Waybar configuration where a ``
    54	leader opens the same four controls as a click-to-reveal drawer from right to
    55	left; external outputs keep the four controls permanently expanded inside the
    56	same pill. It includes an always-visible `` module
    57	backed by interactive `bluetoothctl`, plus an
    58	always-visible custom backlight module backed by `brightnessctl`. Right-clicking
    59	the network icon opens fixed controls for both installer-owned ifupdown
    60	adapters and NetworkManager-owned Ethernet/WiFi devices, plus confirmed MAC
    61	randomization for saved profiles and directly managed physical adapters.
    62	Right-clicking the Bluetooth icon opens quick scan, pair, connect, and
    63	disconnect actions plus nested device and adapter management. BlueZ starts
    64	independently and owns controller policy from `/etc/bluetooth/main.conf`; the
    65	optional hardened
    66	`bluetooth-controller-init.service` runs afterward without overriding rfkill
    67	policy. Its low-level `btmgmt` request is wrapped in an external timeout so the
    68	BlueZ 5.85 client cannot hold the oneshot open. User control continues over the
    69	standard BlueZ D-Bus API on the managed
    70	dbus-broker system bus. KeePassXC is part of the desktop
    71	baseline with a mode-0600 hardened configuration, a private
    72	`~/Syncthing/keepassxc/` database directory, and a native-Wayland launch path
    73	confined by the dedicated no-network KeePassXC AppArmor profile. Avoiding a
    74	second Bubblewrap/AppArmor layer keeps Qt's atomic configuration-file writes
    75	functional while preserving the application-specific filesystem boundary.
    76	Waybar uses the compositor's
    77	native `ext/workspaces` protocol instead of a custom backend. Xwayland remains
    78	excluded by package selection and APT policy; Qt applications and
    79	systemd/D-Bus-activated Qt desktop services use the native Wayland QPA backend,
    80	and the staged Labwc configuration must not include X11 startup assets such as
    81	`xinitrc`. Crystal Dock reads per-desktop configuration from
    82	`~/.config/crystal-dock/labwc/`; the
    83	role also stages the same preset under `/etc/xdg/crystal-dock/labwc/` so
    84	Crystal Dock can copy it on first run when a home directory does not already
